ize the structure to logical zero values, for example:\0A *   \5Ccode\0A *   psa_key_derivation_operation_t operation = {0};\0A *   \5Cendcode\0A * - Initialize the structure to the initializer #PSA_KEY_DERIVATION_OPERATION_INIT,\0A *   for example:\0A *   \5Ccode\0A *   psa_key_derivation_operation_t operation = PSA_KEY_DERIVATION_OPERATION_INIT;\0A *   \5Cendcode\0A * - Assign the result of the function psa_key_derivation_operation_init()\0A *   to the structure, for example:\0A *   \5Ccode\0A *   psa_key_derivation_operation_t operation;\0A *   operation = psa_key_derivation_operation_init();\0A *   \5Cendcode\0A *\0A * This is an implementation-defined \5Cc struct. Applications should not\0A * make any assumptions about the content of this structure.\0A * Implementation details can change in future versions without notice.\0A */\0Atypedef struct psa_key_derivation_s psa_key_derivation_operation_t;\0A\0A/** \5Cdef PSA_KEY_DERIVATION_OPERATION_INIT\0A *\0A * This macro returns a suitable initializer for a key derivation operation\0A * object of type #psa_key_derivation_operation_t.\0A */\0A\0A/** Return an initial value for a key derivation operation object.\0A */\0Astatic psa_key_derivation_operation_t psa_key_derivation_operation_init(void);\0A\0A/** Set up a key derivation operation.\0A *\0A * A key derivation algorithm takes some inputs and uses them to generate\0A * a byte stream in a deterministic way.\0A * This byte stream can be used to produce keys and other\0A * cryptographic material.\0A *\0A * To derive a key:\0A * -# Start with an initialized object of type #psa_key_derivation_operation_t.\0A * -# Call psa_key_derivation_setup() to select the algorithm.\0A * -# Provide the inputs for the key derivation by calling\0A *    psa_key_derivation_input_bytes() or psa_key_derivation_input_key()\0A *    as appropriate. Which inputs are needed, in what order, and whether\0A *    they may be keys and if so of what type depends on the algorithm.\0A * -# Optionally set the operation's maximum capacity with\0A *    psa_key_derivation_set_capacity(). You may do this before, in the middle\0A *    of or after providing inputs. For some algorithms, this step is mandatory\0A *    because the output depends on the maximum capacity.\0A * -# To derive a key, call psa_key_derivation_output_key() or\0A *    psa_key_derivation_output_key_custom().\0A *    To derive a byte string for a different purpose, call\0A *    psa_key_derivation_output_bytes().\0A *    Successive calls to these functions use successive output bytes\0A *    calculated by the key derivation algorithm.\0A * -# Clean up the key derivation operation object with\0A *    psa_key_derivation_abort().\0A *\0A * If this function returns an error, the key derivation operation object is\0A * not changed.\0A *\0A * If an error occurs at any step after a call to psa_key_derivation_setup(),\0A * the operation will need to be reset by a call to psa_key_derivation_abort().\0A *\0A * Implementations must reject an attempt to derive a key of size 0.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam[in,out] operation       The key derivation operation object\0A *                                to set up. It must\0A *                                have been initialized but not set up yet.\0A * \5Cparam alg                     The key derivation algorithm to compute\0A *                                (\5Cc PSA_ALG_XXX value such that\0A *                                #PSA_ALG_IS_KEY_DERIVATION(\5Cp alg) is true).\0A *\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_SUCCESS\0A *         Success.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT\0A *         \5Cc alg is not a key derivation algorithm.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_NOT_SUPPORTED\0A *         \5Cc alg is not supported or is not a key derivation algorithm.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_MEMORY \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_COMMUNICATION_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_HARDWARE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_CORRUPTION_DETECTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_STORAGE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_BAD_STATE\0A *         The operation state is not valid (it must be inactive), or\0A *         the library has not been previously initialized by psa_crypto_init().\0A *         It is implementation-dependent whether a failure to initialize\0A *         results in this error code.\0A */\0Apsa_status_t psa_key_derivation_setup(\0A    psa_key_derivation_operation_t *operation,\0A    psa_algorithm_t alg);\0A\0A/** Retrieve the current capacity of a key derivation operation.\0A *\0A * The capacity of a key derivation is the maximum number of bytes that it can\0A * return. When you get *N* bytes of output from a key derivation operation,\0A * this reduces its capacity by *N*.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam[in] operation     The operation to query.\0A * \5Cparam[out] capacity     On success, the capacity of the operation.\0A *\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_SUCCESS \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_COMMUNICATION_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_HARDWARE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_CORRUPTION_DETECTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_BAD_STATE\0A *         The operation state is not valid (it must be active), or\0A *         the library has not been previously initialized by psa_crypto_init().\0A *         It is implementation-dependent whether a failure to initialize\0A *         results in this error code.\0A */\0Apsa_status_t psa_key_derivation_get_capacity(\0A    const psa_key_derivation_operation_t *operation,\0A    size_t *capacity);\0A\0A/** Set the maximum capacity of a key derivation operation.\0A *\0A * The capacity of a key derivation operation is the maximum number of bytes\0A * that the key derivation operation can return from this point onwards.\0A *\0A * \5Cparam[in,out] operation The key derivation operation object to modify.\0A * \5Cparam capacity          The new capacity of the operation.\0A *                          It must be less or equal to the operation's\0A *                          current capacity.\0A *\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_SUCCESS \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT\0A *         \5Cp capacity is larger than the operation's current capacity.\0A *         In this case, the operation object remains valid and its capacity\0A *         remains unchanged.\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_COMMUNICATION_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_HARDWARE_FAILURE \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_CORRUPTION_DETECTED \5Cemptydescription\0A * \5Cretval #PSA_ERROR_BAD_STATE\0A *         The operation state is not valid (it must be active), or the\0A *         library has not been previously initialized by psa_crypto_init().\0A *         It is implementation-dependent whether a failure to initialize\0A *         results in this error code.\0A */\0Apsa_status_t psa_key_derivation_set_capacity(\0A    psa_key_derivation_operation_t *operation,\0A    size_t capacity);\0A\0A/** Use the maximum possible capacity for a key derivation operation.\0A *\0A * Use this value as the capacity argument when setting up a key derivation\0A * to indicate that the operation should have the maximum possible capacity.\0A * The value of the maximum possible capacity depends on the key derivation\0A * algorithm.\0A */\0A#define PSA_KEY_DERIVATION_UNLIMITED_CAPACITY ((size_t) (-1))\0A\0A/** Provide an input for key derivation or key agreement.\0A *\0A * Which inputs are required and in what order depends on the algorithm.\0A * Refer to the documentation of each key derivation or key agreement\0A * algorithm for information.\0A *\0A * This function passes direct inputs, which is usually correct for\0A * non-secret inputs. To pass a secret input, which should be in a key\0A * object, call psa_key_derivation_input_key() instead of this function.\0A * Refer to the documentation of individual step types\0A * (`PSA_KEY_DERIVATION_INPUT_xxx` value